Task 5 — Create Policy and Verify Access — 20 Marks
This is the most important part of the assessment because you demonstrate Vault ACL authorization.
We will create a policy that allows the devops user to read only:
secret/devops
It will not allow access to other secrets.
Step 19: Create another secret for testing
As root:
vault kv put secret/database \
    username="dbadmin" \
    password="DB@12345"
Now Vault contains:
secret/devops
secret/database
Step 20: Create policy file
Create:
nano devops-policy.hcl
Paste:
path "secret/data/devops" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
Save:
Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X
Why secret/data/devops?
Because you're using KV v2.
The CLI hides the internal /data/ path when you use:
vault kv get secret/devops
But the ACL policy must reference the KV v2 data path. HashiCorp's KV v2 documentation likewise uses the mount/data/... path for ACL rules. �
HashiCorp Developer
Step 21: Upload the policy
Run:
vault policy write devops-policy devops-policy.hcl
Verify:
vault policy list
You should see:
default
devops-policy
root
Step 22: Attach policy to the user
Run:
vault write auth/userpass/users/devops \
    password="Devops@123" \
    policies="devops-policy"
Verify:
vault read auth/userpass/users/devops
You should see the policy.
Step 23: Logout from root
This is important.
Run:
vault logout
Now you are no longer using the root token.
Step 24: Login as devops
Run:
vault login -method=userpass username=devops
Vault will ask:
Password:
Enter:
Devops@123
You should receive a token.
Step 25: Verify the allowed secret
Run:
vault kv get secret/devops
This should work.
You should see:
username
password
environment
✅ This proves the policy works.
Step 26: Test unauthorized access
Now try:
vault kv get secret/database
This should fail with something similar to:
Error making API request.

Code: 403
Errors:

* permission denied
This is exactly what you want.
Your devops user can access:
secret/devops
but cannot access:
secret/database
That demonstrates least-privilege access control.
Vault policies control which paths and operations a token/user can access. �
